// Two interfaces — both define a default move()
interface Swimmer {
    default String move() { return "swim"; }
}

interface Walker {
    default String move() { return "walk"; }
}

// A class CAN implement both interfaces
class Amphibian implements Swimmer, Walker {

    @Override
    public String move() {
        // Must override — Java forces you to resolve the clash
        return Swimmer.super.move() + " and " + Walker.super.move();
    }
}

// This would NOT compile — classes can't multiply inherit
// class D extends B, C { }  // compile error: multiple inheritance not allowed

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Amphibian frog = new Amphibian();
        System.out.println(frog.move()); // swim and walk
    }
}
```

```java
class Animal {
    String name;
    Animal(String name) {       // only a parameterized constructor
        this.name = name;
    }
    // no Animal() no-arg constructor defined
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    Dog() {                     // COMPILE ERROR:
        // compiler tries to insert super() here,
        // but Animal has no no-arg constructor to call
    }
}
```
The error message says something like: *'there is no default constructor available in Animal'*. It appears in `Dog` even though you wrote nothing — because the compiler's invisible `super()` call failed.

```java
class Animal {
    String name;
    Animal(String name) {
        this.name = name;            // parent initialises its own field
    }
}

class Dog extends Animal {
    String breed;

    Dog(String name, String breed) {
        super(name);                 // ← first statement: parent runs first
        this.breed = breed;          // now Dog adds its own state safely
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Dog rex = new Dog("Rex", "Labrador");
        System.out.println(rex.name + " the " + rex.breed);
        // Output: Rex the Labrador
    }
}
```

```java
// IS-A test: 'Car IS-A Engine' → FALSE
class Engine {
    void start() { System.out.println("Engine starting"); }
    void adjustFuelInjection() { /* engine internals */ }
}

class Car extends Engine {   // WRONG: inherits adjustFuelInjection() etc.
    // Car now exposes engine internals to all callers — not what you want
}
```
Forcing inheritance here leaks `Engine`'s entire API into `Car` and makes it nearly impossible to swap the engine type later.

```java
class Engine {
    void start() { System.out.println("Engine starting"); }
}

class Car {                          // Car HAS-A Engine
    private final Engine engine;     // hold it as a field

    Car(Engine engine) {
        this.engine = engine;        // inject it — easy to swap
    }

    void start() {
        engine.start();              // delegate to the Engine
        System.out.println("Car moving");
    }
}

// Swap to ElectricEngine later — Car's callers never notice
class ElectricEngine extends Engine {
    @Override
    void start() { System.out.println("Electric motor starting silently"); }
}
```
